  <h1>How to learn Japanese step by step</h1>
  <p class="lede">Learning Japanese from zero is very doable if you take it in the right order: first the two sound alphabets, hiragana and katakana, then core words and simple grammar, and only then kanji — a little at a time. Japanese grammar is regular and logical, which makes it friendlier than it first looks.</p>

  <div class="ad-slot" role="complementary" aria-label="Advertisement"><span>Advertisement</span></div>

  <h2>Step 1 — Learn hiragana, then katakana</h2>
  <p>Japanese has two alphabets of 46 sounds each. Hiragana is used for Japanese words and grammar endings; katakana is used for foreign words, names and emphasis. Each character stands for one sound, so once you know them, you can read any Japanese word out loud — even before you know what it means.</p>
  <p>Learn hiragana first, in about two weeks of short daily practice, then katakana the same way. Do not skip this step and depend on romaji (Japanese written in English letters). Romaji hides the real sounds and almost no Japanese learning material uses it.</p>
  <h2>Step 2 — Learn your first core words and phrases</h2>
  <p>Start with greetings, numbers, time, food and everyday verbs: こんにちは (konnichiwa, hello), ありがとう (arigatou, thank you), すみません (sumimasen, excuse me). Learn words inside short sentences, for example 私は学生です (watashi wa gakusei desu, I am a student).</p>
  <p>Aim for 5–10 new words a day. Review them the next day, then a few days later — this spaced repetition is what makes words stick.</p>
  <h2>Step 3 — Learn basic grammar: particles and word order</h2>
  <p>Japanese sentences usually follow subject–object–verb order. Small words called particles show the job of each word in the sentence: は (wa) marks the topic, を (wo) marks the object, に (ni) often marks time or direction. For example, 私はごはんを食べます (watashi wa gohan wo tabemasu) means “I eat rice”.</p>
  <p>Learn one particle at a time, with examples, instead of memorising a table of all of them. You will meet them naturally as you read and listen.</p>
  <h2>Step 4 — Start kanji slowly</h2>
  <p>Kanji are characters borrowed from Chinese, and there are thousands of them — but you do not need them all at once. Start with the simplest and most common ones, like 日 (day/sun), 月 (month/moon), 人 (person) and 山 (mountain). Learn each kanji with its meaning and one or two common words it appears in.</p>
  <p>A realistic pace is 3–5 new kanji a day. At that pace you will know several hundred within a few months, which is enough to read simple texts with help.</p>
  <h2>Step 5 — Listen and speak every day</h2>
  <p>Listen to Japanese daily — anime, dramas, podcasts or YouTube — even if you understand only a little. Your ear needs time to get used to the rhythm and sounds. Try shadowing: listen to a short sentence and repeat it immediately, copying the rhythm.</p>
  <p>Speak from early on, even just reading sentences aloud. A tutor is useful here because Japanese has pitch patterns and polite forms that are hard to learn from a book alone.</p>
  <h2>Common mistakes to avoid</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Depending on romaji instead of learning hiragana and katakana.</li>
    <li>Trying to memorise hundreds of kanji before you can make a simple sentence.</li>
    <li>Learning words alone, without the sentences they live in.</li>
    <li>Worrying about being perfect — Japanese speakers appreciate any effort to speak their language.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Practical tips</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Learn hiragana in your first two weeks; it unlocks real Japanese materials.</li>
    <li>Study 20–30 minutes a day, every day, instead of long sessions once a week.</li>
    <li>Learn the polite です/ます forms first — they are the safest way to speak to anyone.</li>
    <li>Keep a small notebook of sentences you want to say, and practise them aloud.</li>
  </ul>

