  <h2>Questions people ask</h2>

  <details><summary>How long does it take to learn Japanese?</summary><p>With about 30 minutes a day, most learners can handle simple everyday conversations after 6–12 months. Reading takes longer because of kanji, but you can read simple texts much sooner if you learn kanji steadily. Reaching a fluent, comfortable level usually takes a few years — and that is normal for Japanese.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Is Japanese hard to learn?</summary><p>Japanese has hard parts: three writing systems and thousands of kanji. But it also has easy parts: pronunciation is very regular, verbs do not change for person, and there are no articles like “a” and “the”. Most learners find the beginning the hardest, and it gets smoother once hiragana, katakana and basic grammar click.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Should I learn kanji from the start?</summary><p>Not from day one. Learn hiragana and katakana and some basic grammar first, then add kanji slowly — a few a day. Kanji learned with real words is far easier to remember than kanji learned from a list alone.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Can I learn Japanese by myself?</summary><p>Yes, for reading, listening and grammar. Speaking is the hard part to do alone, because you need someone to talk to and correct you. A tutor gives you real conversation practice and feedback you cannot get from an app.</p></details>
